window.SLIDE_TEXT["cad-part-1"] = {"1":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Disease Part I:</li><li>Foundations of Coronary Artery Disease</li><li>PHAS 517</li><li>Jessica Stager, MPAS, PA-C</li><li>November 18, 2026</li></ul>",
"2":"<ul><li>Definitions</li><li>ATHEROSCLEROSIS</li><li>Lipid buildup + inflammation → arterial plaque.</li><li>ASCVD</li><li>Atherosclerosis in coronary, brain, limb, or aortic arteries.</li><li>CAD</li><li>Atherosclerosis of the heart’s coronary arteries.</li><li>ASHD</li><li>Atherosclerotic heart disease; usually CAD.</li><li>IHD</li><li>Myocardial oxygen supply falls short of demand.</li><li>CVD</li><li>Heart and blood-vessel diseases; may be nonatherosclerotic.</li><li>Remember: CAD can cause IHD, but ischemia can also occur without obstructive CAD.</li></ul>",
"3":"<ul><li>ATHEROSCLEROSIS</li><li>Lipid accumulation + inflammation within the arterial wall→ formation of atherosclerotic plaque</li><li>ASCVD</li><li>A group of diseases caused by atherosclerosis in different arterial territories</li><li>CORONARY</li><li>Coronary arterydisease (CAD)</li><li>CEREBRAL</li><li>Cerebrovasculardisease</li><li>PERIPHERAL</li><li>Peripheral arterydisease (PAD)</li><li>AORTIC</li><li>Aortic atheroscleroticdisease</li><li>CAD is one manifestation of the broader disease process: ASCVD.</li><li>Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease (ASCVD)</li></ul>",
"4":"<ul><li>Atherosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease (ASCVD)</li><li>Coronary artery disease (CAD), also known as atherosclerotic heart disease (ASHD).</li><li>When this causes symptoms, it will create:<ul><li>Stable Angina</li><li>Acute Coronary Syndrome<ul><li>Unstable Angina</li><li>Non-ST Elevation Myocardial Infarction</li><li>ST-Elevation Myocardial Infarction</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"5":"<ul><li>Arteriosclerotic Cardiovascular Disease Burden</li><li>ASCVD is a major cause of death and disability worldwide.</li><li>ASCVD affects 18.2 million Americans and prevalence increases with age</li><li>Worldwide, ~15.9 million deaths were attributable to ASCVD in 2023</li><li>35 percent of US adults had at least one established risk factor (ie, uncontrolled HTN, dyslipidemia, elevated blood glucose or elevated BMI)</li></ul>",
"6":"<ul><li>ASCVD Risk Factors</li><li>Modifiable</li><li>Hypertension</li><li>Dyslipidemia</li><li>Diabetes mellitus</li><li>Tobacco use</li><li>Obesity</li><li>Physical inactivity</li><li>Unhealthy dietary patterns</li><li>Excessive alcohol use</li><li>Non-Modifiable</li><li>Male sex</li><li>Advancing age</li><li>Family history of premature CAD<ul><li>M &lt; 55 years</li><li>F &lt; 65 years</li></ul></li><li>Important Risk-Enhancing Conditions</li><li>Chronic kidney disease (stage III, IV, V)</li><li>Metabolic syndrome</li><li>Certain chronic inflammatory conditions</li></ul>",
"7":"<ul><li>ASCVD Risk Factors</li><li>The five most common modifiable risk factors (HLD, DM, HTN, obesity, and smoking) are estimated to be responsible for more than 50% of cardiovascular mortality.</li><li>Cigarette smoking increases the risk of ASCVD 2-3x.  That risk decreases 50% within 2 years of quitting.</li><li>Even modest exercise lowers the risk of ASCVD substantially.</li></ul>",
"8":"<ul><li>ASCVD Risk Reduction</li><li>Stop tobacco use<ul><li>Assess tobacco exposure, offer counseling, and provide evidence-based cessation treatment.</li></ul></li><li>Control blood pressure and lipids<ul><li>Identify elevated levels early and use lifestyle measures and medications when indicated.</li></ul></li><li>Promote physical activity and a heart-healthy diet<ul><li>Encourage sustainable changes rather than an all-or-nothing approach.</li></ul></li><li>Manage diabetes and other contributing conditions<ul><li>Individualize treatment according to overall cardiovascular risk and comorbidities.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"9":"<ul><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li><li>Atherosclerosis is a chronic inflammatory process involving lipid accumulation and plaque formation within the arterial wall.</li><li>Lipoproteins enter and accumulate in the intima.</li><li>Inflammatory cells are recruited to the vessel wall.</li><li>Lipid-laden cells form an early lesion called a fatty streak.</li><li>Over time, some lesions progress into more complex atherosclerotic plaques.</li></ul>",
"10":"<ul><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li><li>Atherosclerotic plaques (atheromas), may take 10-15 years for full development.</li><li>The fatty streak- a focal accumulation of serum lipoproteins within the intima of the vessel wall</li></ul>",
"11":"<ul><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li><li>Atherosclerosis progresses at variable rates, with periods of greater and lesser activity.</li><li>Endothelial function is influenced by local blood-flow patterns.</li><li>Atheromas characteristically occur in regions of<ul><li>Branching and marked curvature of vessels</li><li>At areas where the vessel is irregularly shaped</li><li>Where blood undergoes sudden changes in velocity and direction of flow.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"12":"",
"13":"<ul><li>LDL retention and modification</li><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li></ul>",
"14":"<ul><li>Monocyte recruitment and foam cell formation</li><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li></ul>",
"15":"<ul><li>Endothelial dysfunction</li><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li></ul>",
"16":"<ul><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li><li>Nitric oxide (NO)</li><li>Promotes vasodilation and helps inhibit platelet adhesion and vascular inflammation.</li><li>Prostacyclin (PGI₂)</li><li>Promotes vasodilation and inhibits platelet aggregation.</li><li>Key concept: Endothelial dysfunction reduces the artery's normal protective mechanisms and contributes to atherosclerosis.</li></ul>",
"17":"<ul><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li></ul>",
"18":"<ul><li>Plaque disruption and thrombosis</li><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li></ul>",
"19":"<ul><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li><li>As an unruptured plaque ages, it’s inflammation levels gradually decrease, and it becomes progressively more stable with better collagen content in the cap.</li><li>Stable plaques tend to have a dense fibrous cap, minimal lipid accumulation and little inflammation whereas “vulnerable” unstable plaques have thin caps, large lipid cores and dense inflammatory infiltrates.</li></ul>",
"20":"<ul><li>Clinical Consequences</li><li>Plaques that are fairly new with thin fibrous caps are in the process of replacing the collagen fibers in their cap, while active inflammation is present in the lipid pool below the cap.</li><li>Therefore, young plaques are asymptomatic because they don’t block much blood flow, but when they suddenly rupture, they cause acute coronary syndromes, UA/ NSTEMI and STEMI, due to abrupt termination or limitation of blood flow by clot (thrombus) formation.</li></ul>",
"21":"<ul><li>Clinical Consequences</li><li>Older plaques with thick fibrous caps are less likely to rupture but cause narrowing of arterial lumen (stenosis) and limitation of blood flow:<ul><li>Exertional Angina<ul><li>Claudication</li></ul></li></ul></li></ul>",
"22":"<ul><li>Atherosclerosis Pathophysiology</li><li>This entire atherosclerotic pathophysiologic process is accelerated by several factors:<ul><li>Systemic hypertension causing excessive shear forces</li><li>Cigarette smoking</li><li>Hypercholesterolemia</li><li>Inflammation/ oxidative stress</li></ul></li><li>The inflammatory micro-environment  within the walled off lipid plaque is also a pro-clotting, platelet activating environment.</li></ul>",
"23":"<ul><li>STABLE ANGINA/</li><li>CLAUDICATION</li><li>UNSTABLE ANGINA</li><li>ACS/ STROKE (CVA)</li></ul>",
"24":"<ul><li>Clinical Consequences</li><li>Arthur is a 34-year-old man who loves his fast food, but also runs 3 miles a day.  If he suddenly develops chest pain and is found to have an acute MI, what is likely in his arteries?</li><li>Zeke is a 90-year-old man who has been a dairy farmer all his life, and his wife has never tried to feed him any of that “rabbit food”.  If he presents to his PA with intermittent “chest tightness” that prolongs the time it takes to complete his daily chores, what is in his arteries?</li></ul>",
"25":"<ul><li>Positive and Negative Remodeling</li><li>Plaque formation may remodel the coronary artery in one of two ways:</li><li>Positive remodeling occurs when the arterial wall bulges out at the site of the plaque. This is most common and less likely to cause angina initially, though many may change to negative remodeling with plaque progression.</li><li>Negative remodeling occurs when there is no arterial wall dilation, and the plaque narrows the lumen of the blood vessel as it grows.</li></ul>",
"26":"",
"27":"",
"28":"<ul><li>The “Needy” Heart</li><li>The heart has high and continuous metabolic demands.</li><li>Although it makes up only 0.3% of body weight, it accounts for 7% of resting oxygen consumption.</li><li>Coronary blood flow brings oxygen to myocytes and removes waste products such as carbon dioxide, lactic acid, and hydrogen ions.</li></ul>",
"29":"<ul><li>Coronary Blood Flow</li><li>Normal coronary blood flow:</li><li>Blood flow to the myocardium occurs predominantly during diastole.</li><li>During systole, myocardial contraction compresses intramyocardial vessels and reduces blood flow.</li></ul>",
"30":"<ul><li>Coronary Blood Flow</li><li>During exercise:</li><li>Heart rate and contractility increase.</li><li>Myocardial oxygen demand increases.</li><li>Diastole shortens as heart rate rises, reducing the time available for coronary perfusion per beat.</li></ul>",
"31":"<ul><li>Coronary Blood Flow</li><li>How the heart meets demand:</li><li>Normal coronary arteries dilate to increase blood flow.</li><li>With flow-limiting CAD, blood flow may not increase enough to meet demand, resulting in myocardial ischemia.</li></ul>",
"32":"<ul><li>Cardiac Ischemia: Coronary Stenosis</li><li>Coronary atherosclerosis can narrow the coronary arteries and limit blood flow to the myocardium.</li><li>Reduced blood flow limits the heart's ability to increase oxygen delivery when myocardial oxygen demand rises.</li><li>When myocardial oxygen supply cannot meet oxygen demand, myocardial ischemia develops and may cause anginal symptoms.</li></ul>",
"33":"<ul><li>Atherosclerotic coronary disease can cause myocardial ischemia if the fibrous cap over the lipid plaque suddenly ruptures.</li><li>A relatively small plaque, that is not causing significant stenosis at all, can suddenly fissure, especially if the vessel is exposed to significant stress.</li><li>Cardiac Ischemia: Plaque Disruption and Thrombosis</li></ul>",
"34":"<ul><li>Plaque rupture or erosion<ul><li>Thrombogenic material is exposed to the bloodstream</li></ul></li><li>Platelet activation and thrombus formation<ul><li>Coagulation cascade is activated  and thrombus forms at the site of disruption</li></ul></li><li>3.  Reduced coronary blood flow<ul><li>Thrombus obstruct the artery, producing acute myocardial ischemia and potentially myocardial infarction.</li></ul></li><li>Cardiac Ischemia: Plaque Disruption and Thrombosis</li></ul>",
"35":"<ul><li>Cardiac Ischemia</li><li>Cardiac tissue can become ischemic for a variety of reasons- not just ASCVD causing inability of the artery to vasodilate in the setting of increased demand or plaque rupture.<ul><li>Vasospasm- causes Printzmetal’s angina.  Vasospasm may be caused by “twitchy” arterioles or by illicit sympathomimetic drugs creating intense vasoconstriction.</li><li>Congenital lesions- uncommon</li><li>Emboli- septic vegetations from IE</li></ul></li></ul>",
"36":"<ul><li>Cardiac Ischemia</li><li>When myocardial oxygen supply is inadequate, the regional myocardial hypoxia causes anaerobic cellular respiration and production of lactic acid.</li><li>The localized acidosis causes abnormalities in the myocardial relaxation, leading to reduced compliance and contractility and causing regional wall motion abnormalities which are visible on echocardiography.</li><li>Changes of ischemia become visible on the EKG</li></ul>",
"37":"<ul><li>Cardiac Ischemia: Effects on Myocardial Function</li><li>Myocardial stunning</li><li>Occurs after a transient episode of ischemia followed by restoration of blood flow (reperfusion).</li><li>Blood flow has been restored, but myocardial contractility remains temporarily impaired.</li><li>The myocardium is viable; there is no irreversible myocardial injury.</li><li>Recovery may take hours to days, sometimes longer.</li></ul>",
"38":"<ul><li>Cardiac Ischemia: Effects on Myocardial Function</li><li>Myocardial hibernation</li><li>Describes chronically dysfunctional but viable myocardium associated with chronic or repetitive reductions in coronary blood flow.</li><li>The myocardium downregulates its contractile function as an adaptation to reduced oxygen availability.</li><li>Function may improve after coronary blood flow is restored, such as following revascularization.</li></ul>",
"39":"<ul><li>Cardiac Ischemia: Effects on Myocardial Function</li><li>Myocardial infarction</li><li>Prolonged, severe ischemia causes irreversible cardiomyocyte injury and necrosis</li><li>Results in permanent loss of functioning myocardium in the affected region</li></ul>",
"40":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)</li><li>Clinical presentation depends on the disease process:</li><li>Chronic coronary atherosclerosis: may limit blood flow, particularly during exertion, causing myocardial ischemia and angina.</li><li>Acute coronary plaque disruption: may trigger thrombosis and cause acute coronary syndrome (ACS), including unstable angina, NSTEMI, or STEMI.</li></ul>",
"41":"<ul><li>Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)</li></ul>",
"42":"<ul><li>Symptoms of CAD</li><li>Coronary artery disease can cause gradual and intermittent symptoms or can be acutely symptomatic.</li><li>When coronary atherosclerosis causes gradual and intermittent symptoms, this is due to progressively less blood perfusing the myocardium as the plaque grows.</li><li>Symptoms first occur with exertion, then at rest.</li></ul>",
"43":"<ul><li>Symptoms of CAD</li><li>When CAD causes acute symptoms, this is due to plaque rupture.</li><li>Acute symptoms occur abruptly, with no warning anginal symptoms.</li></ul>",
"44":"<ul><li>Symptoms of CAD</li><li>Chest pain: substernal, tight, dull, achy pain that classically radiates to the left shoulder/upper arm.  Does not worsen with deep inspiration or palpation of the sternum.  Lasts minutes to hours.  Women do not always have pain in the classic pattern.</li><li>Dyspnea: worse with exertion</li><li>Nausea</li><li>Dizziness/ Lightheadedness</li><li>Diaphoresis</li></ul>",
"45":"<ul><li>How Are Symptoms Different in Women?</li><li>Women often report their symptoms of cardiac ischemia, but clinicians are less likely to recognize them  as cardiac ischemia symptoms.</li></ul>",
"46":"<ul><li>How Are Symptoms Different in Women?</li><li>In a trial of 515 women who had an acute MI:<ul><li>The most frequently reported symptoms were unusual fatigue, sleep disturbances, SOB, indigestion and anxiety.</li><li>The majority of women (78%) reported at least one symptom for more than 1 month before their heart attack.</li><li>Only 30% reported chest discomfort, which was described as an aching, tightness, pressure, sharpness, burning, fullness or tingling.</li></ul></li></ul>",
"47":"<ul><li>Carl is a 67-year-old man with a history of HTN and diabetes x 20 years.  He moved to the Dayton area 6 months ago and is now coming in to establish primary care at your clinic.</li><li>Carl reports for that for the last 4 months, he has been having a tight sensation in his chest with some shortness of breath when working in the yard.  At first, he thought it was the heat, but it hasn’t improved with cooler weather.  He denies any rest symptoms, nausea, sweats or dizziness.  No cough, weight changes or acid reflux.</li></ul>",
"48":"<ul><li>How Do We Evaluate for CAD?</li><li>Knowing a patient’s risk factors will be an important first step to determining if a patient has coronary artery disease.</li><li>What are Carl’s risk factors?  What additional risk factors do we need to ask about?</li></ul>",
"49":"<ul><li>How Do We Evaluate for CAD?</li><li>A good H&amp;P is essential to begin the evaluation for coronary atherosclerosis.</li><li>In persons who have a significant risk of CAD and have been SYMPTOMATIC, there are tests to further evaluate these patients.</li><li>These tests for cardiac ischemia are not routine or used to screen the general population for CAD- although they have been in the past.  Only persons who are SYMPTOMATIC are screened.</li></ul>",
"50":"<ul><li>How Do We Evaluate for CAD?</li><li>The approach to the evaluation of patients without known CAD who have suspected stable angina depends on the clinical likelihood of obstructive CAD</li><li>The 2024 ESC Risk Factor-weighted Clinical Likelihood (RF-CL) model incorporates:<ul><li>Age and sex<ul><li>Symptom characteristics</li><li>Number of cardiovascular risk factors (smoking, dyslipidemia, hypertension, diabetes, and family history of premature CAD)</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>The model estimates the patient's pretest likelihood of obstructive CAD (%), helping guide the need for further diagnostic testing.</li></ul>",
"51":"",
"52":"<ul><li>Exercise Stress Testing</li><li>Exercise Stress Test (Treadmill Stress Test):</li><li>Appropriate for those who are able to exercise adequately and have a resting EKG that is interpretable for ischemia</li><li>Patient is connected to an electrocardiogram (EKG) and a blood pressure monitor. Patient walks on a treadmill while speed and incline gradually increase every 3 minutes – The Bruce Protocol</li></ul>",
"53":"<ul><li>Exercise Stress Testing</li><li>The Duke Treadmill Score - estimates prognosis and stratifies risk following an exercise treadmill ECG.</li><li>Three variables used:<ul><li>Exercise duration (minutes)</li><li>Maximum ST-segment deviation (mm)</li><li>Angina index (0 = none; 1 = nonlimiting; 2 = exercise-limiting)</li></ul></li></ul><table class=\"ss-table\"><tr><td>Score</td><td>Risk</td></tr><tr><td>≥ +5</td><td>Low</td></tr><tr><td>−10 to +4</td><td>Intermediate</td></tr><tr><td>≤ −11</td><td>High</td></tr></table>",
"54":"<ul><li>Exercise Stress Testing</li><li>Clinical implications:</li><li>Low risk: Generally favorable prognosis; additional testing often unnecessary.</li><li>Intermediate risk: Consider additional evaluation with stress imaging or CCTA.</li><li>High risk: Prompt clinical evaluation; consider invasive coronary angiography depending on clinical findings.</li></ul>",
"55":"<ul><li>Exercise Stress Testing</li><li>EXERCISE STRESS TEST REPORT</li><li>Patient: 58-year-old man with intermittent exertional chest discomfort</li><li>Protocol: Bruce treadmill protocol</li><li>Exercise performance</li><li>Exercise duration: 8 minutes</li><li>Functional capacity: 10 METs</li><li>Maximum heart rate: 150 bpm (approximately 93% of age-predicted maximum)</li><li>Blood pressure: 132/78 mmHg at rest → 178/82 mmHg at peak exercise</li><li>Symptoms</li><li>Developed mild substernal chest pressure during exercise</li><li>Symptoms resolved during recovery</li></ul>",
"56":"<ul><li>Exercise Stress Testing</li><li>ECG findings</li><li>Baseline ECG: Normal sinus rhythm</li><li>At peak exercise: 1 mm horizontal ST-segment depression in leads V4–V6</li><li>ST changes resolved during recovery</li><li>Reason for termination: Fatigue and mild chest discomfort</li><li>Duke Treadmill Score</li><li>-1</li><li>Intermediate risk</li><li>Overall interpretation: Abnormal exercise ECG with exercise-induced ST-segment depression and exertional chest discomfort. Intermediate-risk Duke Treadmill Score.</li></ul>",
"57":"<ul><li>Pharmacologic Stress Testing</li><li>Pharmacologic agents create cardiac stress in two ways:</li><li>1. Coronary vasodilators</li><li>Adenosine</li><li>Regadenoson (Lexiscan) – most common</li><li>Dipyridamole (Persantine)</li><li>Dilate coronary arterioles, increasing blood flow in healthy coronary territories more than in territories supplied by significantly stenosed arteries.</li><li>2. Increased myocardial oxygen demand</li><li>Dobutamine (stress echo)</li><li>Increases heart rate and contractility, raising myocardial oxygen demand to simulate the effects of exercise.</li></ul>",
"58":"<ul><li>Pharmacologic vs Exercise Stress</li><li>Who gets which stress test?</li><li>Rule #1: Acute MI/ ongoing cardiac ischemia is a contraindication to stress testing of any type.</li><li>Rule #2: An exercise stress test is generally preferred, however if the patient cannot exercise adequately due to joint disease or deconditioning/ obesity, it will not be able to be done.</li></ul>",
"59":"<ul><li>Who Gets Which Stress Test?</li><li>Rule #3: Baseline EKG abnormalities (like a LBBB or ventricular pacing) – pharmacologic stress test with imaging</li><li>Rule #4: Vasodilators should not be used in:<ul><li>Hypotension</li><li>High degree AV blocks</li><li>Sick sinus syndrome</li><li>Active wheezing, may cause exacerbation of bronchospastic airway disease (COPD, asthma)</li></ul></li></ul>",
"60":"",
"61":"<ul><li>Radionucleotide Myocardial Perfusion Imaging (MPI)</li><li>Purpose</li><li>Evaluates myocardial blood flow at rest and during stress.</li><li>Identifies areas of reduced perfusion that may indicate myocardial ischemia.</li><li>Helps assess the extent and severity of perfusion abnormalities.</li><li>When is MPI particularly useful?</li><li>Baseline ECG abnormalities that make exercise ECG interpretation unreliable (e.g., LBBB).</li><li>Patients unable to exercise adequately.</li><li>Patients requiring additional assessment of myocardial perfusion.</li></ul>",
"62":"<ul><li>How Stress Testing With MPI Is Done</li><li>How is the test performed?</li><li>Patient receives exercise or pharmacologic stress.</li><li>A radioactive tracer (radiopharmaceutical) is injected intravenously during stress.</li><li>A gamma camera obtains images of myocardial tracer distribution.</li><li>Rest and stress images are compared to identify perfusion defects.</li></ul>",
"63":"<ul><li>How Stress Testing With MPI Is Done</li><li>Reversible ischemia: areas of darkness in the exercise images (no tracer) but perfused with tracer in the resting images.</li></ul>",
"64":"<ul><li>Stress Testing with MPI</li><li>Pregnancy is the only contraindication to the use of MPI because of the radioactivity in the tracer.</li><li>The stress test is read by a specially trained cardiologist who will report on each section separately, and often will calculate an EF.</li><li>A stress test is positive if there is evidence of myocardial ischemia on ECG or imaging</li></ul>",
"65":"<ul><li>Other Forms of Cardiac Stress Testing</li><li>Stress Echocardiography</li><li>A baseline resting echo is obtained first.  Then, stress is induced with exercise (treadmill or bicycle) or with medications (dobutamine).</li><li>Echocardiography is used to evaluate for wall motion abnormalities induced by ischemia</li><li>Stress echo using dobutamine can also evaluate the viability of hibernating myocardium.</li></ul>",
"66":"<ul><li>Cardiac Stress Testing: Appropriate Pts</li><li>When a patient has symptoms suggestive of angina and the patient has an intermediate or high pre-test probability of CAD.</li><li>A patient who had a recent acute coronary syndrome (UA or NSTEMI) if the patient was treated conservatively without a left heart cath- in these cases, a stress test is used to risk stratify the patient</li></ul>",
"67":"<ul><li>Cardiac Stress Testing: Appropriate Pts</li><li>The patient has a known history of coronary artery disease and develops worsening symptoms or new symptoms.</li><li>The patient has newly diagnosed HFrEF or cardiomyopathy, if a cardiac catheterization is not planned.</li><li>A patient with active cardiac conditions presents for pre-op evaluation before a non-cardiac elective surgery, and evaluation is indicated.</li></ul>",
"68":"<ul><li>Cardiac Stress Testing: Inappropriate Pts</li><li>Active chest pain or other symptoms of acute coronary syndromes- UA, NSTEMI.</li><li>Patients with prior coronary revascularization and no new or changed symptoms &lt; 5 years after CABG or &lt; 2 years after PCI with stent.</li><li>Patients without symptoms of coronary disease. (with a few rare exceptions)</li></ul>",
"69":"<ul><li>Cardiac Stress Testing: Inappropriate Pts</li><li>Patients with acutely decompensated heart failure or uncontrolled arrhythmias.</li><li>Patients with advanced/ critical aortic stenosis.</li><li>Patients with pulmonary embolus or infective endocarditis.</li><li>Patients with medical illness that may be aggravated by stress testing – thyrotoxicosis, acute systemic illness/infection.</li></ul>",
"70":"<ul><li>CCTA (Coronary CT Angiography)</li><li>What it does</li><li>Uses IV contrast to visualize coronary arteries, plaque, and luminal narrowing.</li><li>When it is useful</li><li>Stable chest pain with no previously established CAD, particularly intermediate clinical likelihood.</li><li>Can clarify inconclusive or abnormal stress test results.</li><li>Limitations</li><li>Radiation exposure and iodinated contrast.</li><li>Image quality may be reduced by heavy coronary calcification or irregular heart rhythms.</li><li>May identify incidental findings.</li></ul>",
"71":"",
"72":"",
"73":"<ul><li>For individuals with known moderate or severe coronary calcification, we prefer stress testing over CCTA because calcium can obscure the lumen of coronary arteries, making quantification of stenosis difficult.</li><li>For patients &gt;65 years of age, we prefer stress testing over CCTA because these patients are likely to have coronary calcification.</li><li>For individuals with chronic kidney disease, we prefer stress testing over CCTA because there is a risk of contrast-induced acute kidney injury.</li><li>CCTA (Coronary CT Angiography)</li></ul>",
"74":"<ul><li>CAC Scoring</li><li>Noncontrast CT measures calcified plaque burden in the coronary arteries.</li><li>Reported as an Agatston score.</li><li>A score of 0 means no detectable calcified plaque, but does not exclude noncalcified plaque or CAD.</li><li>Used selectively to refine primary-prevention decisions when the need for lipid-lowering treatment is uncertain.</li><li>A higher score indicates greater calcified plaque burden and generally higher ASCVD risk.</li></ul>",
"75":"<ul><li>CAC Scoring</li></ul>",
"76":"<ul><li>Coronary Angiography</li><li>What is it?</li><li>Coronary angiography uses contrast dye and fluoroscopy to visualize coronary arteries and identify stenosis or occlusion.</li><li>Invasive coronary angiography is the reference standard for defining coronary anatomy.</li><li>Allows for intervention (e.g., PCI with stent placement) when indicated.</li></ul>",
"77":"<ul><li>1. Vascular access</li><li>Radial (wrist) or femoral (groin) artery is accessed.</li><li>A small introducer sheath is inserted.</li><li>2. Catheter advancement</li><li>A catheter is advanced through the artery into the aorta under fluoroscopic guidance.</li><li>For coronary angiography, the catheter is positioned at the openings (ostia) of the coronary arteries.</li><li>3. Coronary angiography</li><li>Iodinated contrast is injected into the coronary arteries.</li><li>Fluoroscopy captures images showing coronary anatomy and areas of narrowing or blockage.</li><li>Coronary Angiography</li></ul>",
"78":"",
"79":"<ul><li>A short film clip is made of the procedure.</li><li>Normal RCA in A and normal LAD (in front) and left circumflex arteries on the side (B)</li><li>Coronary Angiography</li></ul>",
"80":"<ul><li>Risks:<ul><li>Bleeding, hematoma, or vascular injury at the access site<ul><li>Contrast-associated acute kidney injury or allergic reaction</li><li>Radiation exposure</li><li>Arrhythmias, MI, stroke, or other serious complications (uncommon)</li></ul></li></ul></li><li>Coronary Angiography</li></ul>",
"81":"<ul><li>As dye is flowing through the coronary artery, the cardiologist determines the degree of stenosis present, expressed as a percentage and the location.</li><li>For example:<ul><li>Left main: 30% mid vessel lesion</li><li>LAD: 60% ostial lesion, 50% distal lesion</li><li>LCx: totally occluded at mid vessel</li><li>RCA: 30% mid-distal vessel lesion</li></ul></li><li>Coronary Angiography</li></ul>",
"82":"<ul><li>A left ventriculogram may be performed at the end the LHC to estimate the EF and measure the pressure in the left heart.</li><li>To do this, the cardiologist will have the catheter cross the aortic valve into the LV and then inject dye, estimating how much is ejected.</li><li>Coronary Angiography</li></ul>",
"83":"<ul><li>The procedure lasts about an hour, and then pressure is held to prevent bleeding from the arterial puncture.  A closure device may also be used if a femoral arterial puncture is performed.</li><li>Normal left ventriculogram during a left heart catheterization</li><li>A: Diastole</li><li>B: Systole</li><li>https://www.researchgate.net</li><li>Coronary Angiography</li></ul>",
"84":"<ul><li>Coronary angiography can be diagnostic and therapeutic.</li><li>If significant coronary artery narrowing is identified, the cardiologist may perform percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) during the same procedure.</li><li>PCI restores coronary blood flow using balloon angioplasty and, commonly, placement of a coronary stent.</li><li>How PCI works:</li><li>Balloon catheter advanced across the narrowed segment.</li><li>Balloon inflated to compress plaque against the arterial wall and expand the vessel.</li><li>Stent expanded to help maintain vessel patency and restore blood flow.</li><li>Coronary Angiography</li></ul>",
"85":"<ul><li>A cardiac cath may be more than diagnostic, it may be therapeutic as well, and an angioplasty with stent placement performed during the LHC.</li><li>A PCI- percutaneous coronary intervention or PTCA- percutaneous coronary angioplasty.</li><li>Coronary Angiography</li></ul>",
"86":"<ul><li>Angioplasty- Before and After</li><li>Coronary Artery Disease 2024, Part I</li></ul>"};
